{"name":"Fire Engine","hex":"#fe0002","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fire engine","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A vehicle used by firefighters to pump water to fight a fire. Typically, a fire engine carries a supply of water and has the ability to connect to an external water supply.","A steam engine."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fire_engine"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fire engine","blocks":[{"text":"A fire engine is a vehicle, usually a specially designed or modified truck, that functions as a firefighting apparatus. The primary purposes of a fire engine include transporting firefighters and water to an incident as well as carrying equipment for firefighting operations in a fire drill. Some fire engines have specialized functions, such as wildfire suppression and aircraft rescue and firefighting, and may also carry equipment for technical rescue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Dublin_Fire_Brigade_Pump_Ladder_D32.jpg/330px-Dublin_Fire_Brigade_Pump_Ladder_D32.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fire_engine"},{"title":"Fire","blocks":[{"text":"Fire is the rapid oxidation of a fuel in the exothermic chemical process of combustion, releasing heat, light, and various reaction products.\nFlames, the most visible portion of the fire, are produced in the combustion reaction when the fuel reaches its ignition point temperature. Flames from hydrocarbon fuels consist primarily of carbon dioxide, water vapor, oxygen, and nitrogen. If hot enough, the gases may become ionized to produce plasma. The color and intensity of the flame depend on the type of fuel and composition of the surrounding gases."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Large_bonfire.jpg/330px-Large_bonfire.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fire"},{"title":"Engine","blocks":[{"text":"An engine or motor is a machine designed to convert one or more forms of energy into mechanical energy."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/dc/4StrokeEngine_Ortho_3D_Small.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Engine"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Humberside_Fire_%26_Rescue_DH03P4_-_YT21_EHF.jpg/500px-Humberside_Fire_%26_Rescue_DH03P4_-_YT21_EHF.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Humberside_Fire_%26_Rescue_DH03P4_-_YT21_EHF.jpg/1280px-Humberside_Fire_%26_Rescue_DH03P4_-_YT21_EHF.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Humberside_Fire_%26_Rescue_DH03P4_-_YT21_EHF.jpg/500px-Humberside_Fire_%26_Rescue_DH03P4_-_YT21_EHF.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A Scania P280 fire engine used by the Humberside Fire and Rescue Service in England","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHumberside_Fire_%26_Rescue_DH03P4_-_YT21_EHF.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Front_of_a_MAN_fire_engine_with_winch_and_shackles.jpg/500px-Front_of_a_MAN_fire_engine_with_winch_and_shackles.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Front_of_a_MAN_fire_engine_with_winch_and_shackles.jpg/500px-Front_of_a_MAN_fire_engine_with_winch_and_shackles.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Front of a MAN fire engine with built-in winch, e.g. for towing damaged cars. The shackles serve a similar purpose.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFront_of_a_MAN_fire_engine_with_w